                <pb facs="0693" n="583" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg583"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/5860: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Eberhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-07-25T19:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">July 25, 1928—7
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>295. General Feland reports that five aeroplanes returned today from region
                    described in Legation’s 292, July 21, 3 p.m., reporting <pb facs="0694" n="584"
                        xml:id="pg584"/> that they were again subjected to machine gun and rifle
                    fire, three aeroplanes being hit by rifle fire but not seriously damaged. They
                    returned the fire, but results not yet reported and difficult to ascertain clue
                    to dense forest. This occurred at a lumber camp owned by a German citizen which
                    the outlaws had seized and were occupying.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Eberhardt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
